2026-08-30 (relaunch #18) **INCREMENT 2 FULLY GATED: THE OBJECT-STATE WRITE MODEL — the KNOWN, QUANTIFIED FRAME-HONESTY GAP IS CLOSED, and the frame plane is now CHECKED rather than assumed.** ALL THREE affected mirrors RE-PROVED SEQUENTIALLY: `frontend/Module3_Weaver` **259/259 Valid, SUCCESS**, `module6_whyml/functions` **1191/1191 Valid, SUCCESS** (1187 was the baseline, +4 goals), `frontend/pure_ast` **2857/2857 Valid, SUCCESS** (~50 min), 0 non-Valid each, 0 emitted axioms in all three — LEDGER STAYS 3. WHAT WAS WRONG: an abstract `val setattr_* … : unit` carries NO effect in Why3, so EVERY mutating body satisfied `#@ assigns \nothing` VACUOUSLY — the frame clause of a mutating method was TRUE OF THE MODEL and FALSE OF THE SOURCE. WHAT IS TRUE NOW: one abstract program variable `val _pyobj_state : ref int` that every object-state write TOUCHES; every `setattr_*` val declares `writes { _pyobj_state }`; and `_emit_function` emits the matching frame clause once the emitted body is known to store an attribute — `writes { }` when the source says `\nothing` (so Why3 REJECTS it: "this expression depends on variable _pyobj_state, which is left out in the specification"), the merged `writes { …, _pyobj_state }` when the source names any target. NINE mirror methods were re-annotated honestly and re-proved: `PyCSLWeaver._init_function_csl_fields` (31 stores; `#@ assigns node`), `_Parser._fin_pos` (4; `assigns node`), `_Unparser.block`/`visit`/`visit_Module`/`visit_Try`/`visit_TryStar` (`assigns self._indent` / `self._source` / `self._type_ignores` / `self._in_try_star` x2), `FunctionEmissionMixin._refine_tuple_return_type` (4) and `_build_param_list`. THE CELL IS DELIBERATELY COARSE — one cell for every object and every attribute — because a frame may OVER-approximate what changes and must never UNDER-approximate it; and attribute READS deliberately stay effect-free `val`s with NO `reads` clause, so two reads of the same attribute remain UNRELATED. That is precisely the `val function` projector purity the `_desugar_for` build was DECLINED for, and this build does NOT reintroduce it — the two halves are separable and only the sound one is spent. THE RECORDED GAP SAID ELEVEN METHODS; IT IS NINE: the two `statements.py` entries were FALSE POSITIVES of the earlier census, which matched the emitter mirror's own STRING LITERALS `"val setattr_"` instead of an emitted application — the same substring-vs-application trap as lesson "detect by LINE PREFIX, never substring", one level down in the emitted `.mlw`. PLACEMENT WAS THE WHOLE ENGINEERING PROBLEM AND IT IS A NEW LESSON: the five `setattr` emission sites live in `statements.py`, whose bodies are MIRRORED UN-TRUSTED, so the first cut (a `_register_setattr_op` helper there) immediately cost TWO NEW `DIVERGED` fidelity entries, a ported helper method, and an unbound `_obj_state_written` writes label. Moving the same three lines into `_add_abstract_op` — which is a `\trusted` mirror stub, i.e. INVISIBLE to the fidelity plane — landed the identical capability with ZERO mirror churn. **WHEN A LIVE-EMITTER CHANGE COSTS FIDELITY DRIFT, LOOK FOR A `\trusted`-MIRRORED CHOKE POINT THAT THE SAME CHANGE CAN SIT BEHIND.** GATES, all driver-verified fresh: mirror sweep WITH L3-tc **3 of 52** changed, TC_FAIL 0; corpus byte-diff **0 of 814** (setattr appears in 0 corpus files — measured, not assumed); fidelity **2 DIVERGED** (baseline `_handle_var_expr`/`_handle_for_stmt`); mirror-check 3 drifted exit 1 (baseline); check-untrusted-emitted **817/801/0/0** (baseline); shadowed-selfcalls **14 methods / 153 sites, ratchet 14 HELD**; emitted-vacuity `--emit` no NEW erasure, 8 known, 0 input-blind; doc-coherency GREEN (no new directive — `#@ assigns <name>` was already grammatical). COUNT UNCHANGED at markers **492** / grep **517**: this is a FAITHFULNESS increment, and it is the first one to make a FRAME claim checkable rather than a value claim.
2026-08-30 (relaunch #18) **CERTIFIED-BOUNDARY RE-MEASURED AND ITS RECORDED REASON CORRECTED FOR THE FOURTH TIME RUNNING: the "attribute store is STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE in Why3" refutation was WRONG ABOUT ITS OWN HORNS.** The record said the two horns are EXCLUSIVE AND EXHAUSTIVE — either the store is a `ref (map ...)` (inherently GHOST, so attribute values cannot flow into non-ghost computation) or it is an `array int` and `get_<attr>` becomes state-dependent ("depends on external variables, cannot be used as pure") and therefore illegal in every contract clause. **THERE IS A THIRD HORN AND IT WORKS: PASS THE STATE AS AN ARGUMENT.** Spiked directly on Why3 in minutes (`scratchpad/w2/heapspike.mlw`): `val _pyobj_state : ref int` (an opaque HEAP TOKEN, a program value, not a map, so not ghost); `val function get_attr (st: int) (o: int) (f: int) : int` — PURE, because the state is an ARGUMENT and not an external variable, hence legal in every contract clause; `val setattr_3 (x f v: int) : unit writes { _pyobj_state } ensures { get_attr !_pyobj_state x f = v }`; and `val getattr_2 (x f) ensures { result = get_attr !_pyobj_state x f }`. MEASURED WITH Alt-Ergo: the mutate-then-read TRUE fact `ensures result = 1` after `node.f = 1` is **Valid**; the FALSE preservation claim `get_attr !st node f = get_attr (old !st) node f` across a store is **Unknown** — i.e. exactly the unsoundness the `_desugar_for` decline named is GONE, and the fact a faithful model must supply IS derivable. **SO WHY IS IT STILL A BOUNDARY? A NEW AND MUCH MORE PRECISE REASON: OBJECT-IDENTITY INJECTIVITY.** The relation `get_attr st o f = v` is only sound if two DISTINCT Python objects never lower to the SAME Why3 term. In the int-erased object model they can: `_coerce_to_int` collapses an unmodelled object expression to the LITERAL `0`, and an int-erased local is emitted as `ref 0`, so two distinct objects can be PROVABLY EQUAL and a write to one would then be readable from the other. Verified against the emitted `.mlw` rather than assumed: on TODAY's mirror surface every `setattr_*`/`getattr_*` object term is in fact a plain identifier (`node` 35 / `self` 12 writes, `self` 22 / typed `x` 8 reads) and never a literal — so the hazard is not currently EXERCISED — but nothing in the emitter PREVENTS it, and the campaign's acceptance test is "does the model claim anything false", not "does it happen to be safe today". **REOPENING CAPABILITY, now exact: object identity must be INJECTIVE — either the AST/record model that gives objects distinct structured values (ladder item 4), or an abstract allocator that mints provably-distinct references. The heap encoding itself is DONE and costs no axiom.** Note also that this is the SOUND half that pairs with increment 2: increment 2 landed the WRITE effect (which needs no injectivity, because a coarse frame only over-approximates); this READ RELATION is the half that does.
